Wrapping at the relay boundary.

**Build 0.11.412** (`2C85D70AD8070513B81D772`, was `93FB14589298C2E12BBAE2D`). Clean compile.

**The "do not board" status is over.** Eleven versions (0.11.402..412) had gone unboarded because each session deferred the rig cycle to land one more fix. I broke that first, and it cost four minutes:

| criterion | result |
|---|---|
| `fence_during_write` | PASS 32/32, 8/8 checks, **17s/60s** |
| `crash_consistency` | PASS 32/32, **204/204**, **86s/90s** |
| `dir_reuse_coherency` | PASS 32/32, 93/93, **107s/120s** |

`crash_consistency` is the thinnest-margin cell on the board and it's green. `dir_reuse_coherency`, the board's only FAIL, had timed out at 120/120s under `hostload=32.60` and passes at 19.26 — load-correlated, so it's the existing pace defect, and per RULE 6 a clean run is not a disposition; it stays open.

**Fixed a real shipped defect.** The sess68 audit item was correct and worse than described: `mxfs_disklock_purge_node` published by plain `read_sector`+`write_sector`. Six call sites, and every survivor runs one on peer death, so concurrent purgers are the normal case. A delayed second purge zeroes a slot a *live* node has since claimed — destroying its grant, or, on the heartbeat sector, erasing a live node's record so the cluster fences and replays a node that is still writing. I also found a second hole in the same function: the freeze gate was decided before the 65536-record scan but the zero pass re-checked only "does the descriptor name this victim," not stage/owner/torn — so a descriptor that moved during the scan got published anyway. Both are now exact-image CAS with the gate re-derived on the image being published.

**The finding that outweighs all of it.** I went to verify that GPT's required "completed fence" is producible. It isn't: `mxfs_scsipr_fence_node()` returns 0 for *"fenced, never there, or advisory topology"*, and on this rig every VM shares one host I_T nexus, so per-node PR is unusable. The RULE-5 ruling on that is unambiguous, and it moves the problem out of the unbuilt coordinator into shipped code:

> If V has not been excluded, replaying V's journal while V can still write is unsafe.

So the live path — peer dies, `fence_node()` returns 0, record stage `FENCED`, replay the slice in place — is doing in-place XFS replay against a node that may still be writing, and `FENCED` is a durable on-disk claim of a guarantee never obtained. GPT also killed the software-only escape I was hoping for: gating bulk replay writes on `owner_term` is not constructible, because a C&W on the descriptor cannot condition a write to an unrelated LBA. The honest answer is fail closed, with real fencing escape hatches — and an explicit product decision about whether advisory-PR hardware supports automatic recovery at all.

Both rulings are recorded in ccmemory with the P0-P4 order. Next session starts by ledgering three defects (purge publication — fixed, verification owed; `FENCED` without proven exclusion — critical; victim replay without proven exclusion — critical) and implementing P0.

**Criteria: NO.** Not production ready. The board is green, but green boards are not the bar — 14 defects remain open, 7 critical, and this session added evidence for two more, one of which is a live corruption risk on the shipped recovery path.

